          "case_name": "In re Bailey M.",
          "citation": "2002 ME 12, 788 A.2d 590",
          "court": "Supreme Judicial Court of Maine",
          "year": 2002,
          "date_decided": "2002-01-28",
          "holding": "The Law Court affirmed denial of a mother's motion to open her termination proceeding to the public, holding that 22 M.R.S. § 4007(1) presumptively closes child protection proceedings and records and that the mother lacked standing to assert the public's First Amendment right of access.",
          "relevance": "Fatality review of a death in care runs up against the presumption of closure recognized here, and the Court tied that presumption to CAPTA's confidentiality conditions at 42 U.S.C. § 5106a(b)(4).",
          "quote": "the Legislature intended section 4007(1) to mean that child protection proceedings are presumptively closed",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2077801/in-re-bailey-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2077801
        },
        {
          "case_name": "J.R. v. Gloria",
          "citation": "593 F.3d 73 (1st Cir. 2010)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the First Circuit",
          "year": 2010,
          "date_decided": "2010-01-27",
          "holding": "The First Circuit affirmed qualified immunity for child-welfare caseworkers sued for failing to protect twins abused in a licensed foster home, holding that even assuming a special relationship arose from foster placement, the plaintiffs must show conscience-shocking conduct and their evidence did not show even deliberate indifference.",
          "relevance": "The court's framework explains why fatality and serious-injury reviews rarely translate into successful federal claims in this circuit.",
          "quote": "Even then, the claim against the defendants must also involve \"conscience-shocking\" conduct by state officials",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/204155/jr-v-gloria/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 204155
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Connor B. ex rel. Vigurs v. Patrick",
          "citation": "774 F.3d 45 (1st Cir. 2014)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the First Circuit",
          "year": 2014,
          "date_decided": "2014-12-15",
          "holding": "The First Circuit affirmed judgment for the state in a systemic foster-care class action, holding the record did not establish a class-wide substantive due process violation or a class-wide failure to maintain individualized case plans under the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act.",
          "relevance": "Editorial findings of agency deficiency by a trial court, short of constitutional error, are directed to the political branches for correction.",
          "quote": "We agree with the district court that this record does not show a class-wide failure to provide documentation in the form of individualized case plans.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2760820/connor-b-ex-rel-vigurs-v-patrick/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2760820
        }
